essay:
Kittler on the Roof held the number one ranking for exactly seven days.

One week. That's how long the defending champion sat on the throne before MHJTIME — the same 1-3 team getting eulogized a fortnight ago — walked in and dropped 140.94 on the crown. Rico Dowdle went for 30.9. Ja'Marr Chase added 20.1. Jayden Daniels orchestrated with 22.64. And Kittler's 107.74 — a score that would've lost to eight of the twelve teams this week — sent the defending champion tumbling from first to fifth in the most dramatic single-week fall the power rankings have recorded. It was a prestige-TV collapse: the slow build to the top, the one-week reign, the sudden freefall while the credits roll.

{{media:week6-essay-opener}}

Last week, five teams shared a penthouse at 4-1 and we called it unprecedented parity. This week, three of them separated at 5-1, and both of the other two dropped out of the top four entirely. So who filled the vacuum at the top? The Legion of Bouz reclaimed the number one spot with 165.9 — Mahomes and Cam Skattebo each eclipsed 30 points — while General Ken-obi stormed from fifth to second behind a 164.94 demolition of Burden that featured the week's most absurd stat line: the Rams defense scored 27.0 points. A defense. Twenty-seven. Drake London added 26.8, Drake Maye dropped 26.24, and the franchise everyone measures themselves against is suddenly measuring everybody else from the peak of its all-time Elo at 1700.2.

Meanwhile, the group chat turned into a trade bazaar. Brent opened the market with a casual "Hit me up if in need of." Karim countered with "2/3 top QBs come at me." Harlow pitched "2 of the top 3 RBs on the Chiefs." Zach offered "Buncha old serviceable WR3/4s here." And then Ben delivered the line of the week: "If you want some old serviceable WR3/4s with championship pedigree instead of Zach's 2nd place also rans, let me know." Brent's verdict — "Zach got served by Ben" — was the only punctuation that moment needed. The trade deadline hasn't arrived, but the desperation has.

{{media:week6-trade-bazaar}}

The Sleeping Giants kept sleepwalking to wins — four straight now, Josh Jacobs' 29.5 doing the heavy lifting against a Chudders squad that managed just 95.26. The Boonist Monks bounced back from their Kittler loss with a 150.46 explosion that saw Pickens and JSN combine for 53.5 points. And at the bottom, Burden and Father Time remain winless at 0-6, staring at each other across the standings like castaways on the same sinking raft — fitting, because next week they play each other, and somebody finally has to win.

But forget the bottom of the standings for a second. Circle next week in permanent marker: The Legion of Bouz versus General Ken-obi. First versus second. Both 5-1. Two former champions with two titles apiece. Mahomes versus Maye. The Jailyard doesn't do regular-season Super Bowls — until it does.

